# The UI contract comes first: the model, then the screen, then the code

## Pyramid Layer

- Layer: leaf (presentation, tests, the modeling thread), reached from `process-check-rules-skills.mdc`; its procedure is the skill `.agents/skills/adding-ui-anchors/SKILL.md`.

## Use This When

- A view gains, renames or loses an anchor, a code, a state, a choice value, a control, a list, a dialog or a screen.
- A file under `contract/` or `tests/e2e/scenarios/` changes, an e2e test names the contract, or a test or an agent cannot find an element.

## Stop Here If

- `bun run contract:check`, `contract:check:sources`, `contract:check:consumers`, `e2e:coverage` and `contract:coverage:check` are green, `bun run contract:coverage --unmarked` lists no unmarked control, and the scenario that reaches the element has its test.

## Descend To

- The steps, the patterns of our kit and views, the checklists and the templates: `.agents/skills/adding-ui-anchors/SKILL.md` and its `references/`. The closed lists: `contract/vocabulary.md`, never copied. The folder for people: `contract/README.md`.
- Visible text and `aria-label` keys: `ui-i18n-direct-keys.mdc`. The controls: `ui-primitive-hygiene.mdc`.

## Core rule

- **Model first.** Completeness comes from `contract/domain/model.schema.json`: a screen unfolds its entities, the anchors bind their fields and actions, and the DOM, the tests and the scenarios are checked back against the contract. A change starts in the model, then the screen file, then the scenario, then the code and the tests; an anchor, a code, a state or a route never appears first in JSX or in a test.
- **Who decides.** The developer programs the architecture in the contract, apart from the implementation: the model, the screens with their flows, the platform, the activities and the scenarios, every architectural decision taken there. The agent brings the patterns that make the contract good, proposed for the developer's review with their trade-offs, and builds the implementation from the contract the developer approved. The code takes no architectural decision of its own: a question the code raises goes back to the contract and its review.
- **Two layers, three kinds of commit.** The contract has a business layer and a meta layer. The business layer is the entry point of every business feature: `contract/domain/` (the screens, the activities, the platform, the model), the scenarios of `tests/e2e/scenarios/`, and the content tables of `contract/vocabulary.md` (the codes, the states, the choice values, the screen names, the roles, the shell's reserved anchors): a row of them travels with the screen file that first uses it. The meta layer is the engine that keeps the contract working: `@workspace/ui-contract` (its schemas, checks, manifest, runtime helpers, and `./machines`, the walk that runs every flow and activity in XState inside `contract:check` and the comparison an app holds its own machines to their flows with), the generated `contract/schemas/`, the grammar of `contract/vocabulary.md` (its Grammar section, the kinds, the derived suffixes, the attributes, the contract version), `contract/README.md`, the rules and skills about the contract, the contract steps of the pre-commit hook, the prose of `COVERAGE.md` and the contract's rows of `docs/agents/context-smoke-test.md`. An app is one consumer of the engine, a particular case: its code and its tests are the implementation, its runtime of the contract (`window.__perch.ui`, the served manifest) and the specs that hold it to the contract included (the web app's `apps/web/src/platform/machines.spec.ts` with its `APP_MACHINES`, `routes-*.spec.ts`, the e2e spec of the manifest), and so is an example in a meta doc that quotes the app's code, which changes in the implementation commit that changes that code. A meta commit touches an app only where the app consumes what the engine changed (an import path, a call), never what the app does. On a branch a commit holds one of the three, never two. A feature that needs a new facet lands the meta change first (the engine learns it), then commits the business change that uses it. The generated block of `COVERAGE.md` goes with whichever commit changed what it measures, on an area branch: it measures the code too, so the contract trunk leaves it to the implementation that follows the take; `contract:coverage:check` fails a block that is not what `contract:coverage --write` writes, in `bun run check` and in the hook on `main`.
- **Branches**, by `branch-reuse-first.mdc`, whose example this is. The meta layer's area is the contract engine, and its branch is `feat/contract-first-pass`: a new round first merges the latest `main` into it, then takes its meta commits, and the branch lands on `main` as a squash of its own after the developer's go on its hand-over, which names the meta commits by hash and the business change that will use them, with the engine's specs showing the facet in use. The go covers exactly those commits: one round is open at a time, and a meta commit added after the hand-over, another feature's included, needs a new hand-over of the whole round before the squash. A change that hand-over's review brings is, as in the business review below, a commit of its own on top, never an amend or a re-cut. A feature's business layer is modelled on the contract trunk, `contract/trunk` (`branch-reuse-first.mdc`), once the meta it needs has landed and the trunk has merged `main`; the feature's own branch takes the card's trunk commits as one business commit, its implementation commits go on top, and it lands on `main` as its own squash. A facet found missing in the middle of a feature takes the same road: its meta goes on `feat/contract-first-pass` and lands, `main` is merged into the trunk and into the feature branch (the contract commits already taken are never rebased), and the business commit that uses it is modelled on the trunk and taken like the first.
- **Review gate.** A change to what the app shows or does runs in two phases. First the contract: the business layer (the model, the screen file with its anchors, facets and flow's states, events, guards and effects, the platform, the activities and the scenarios), on the contract trunk, after the meta layer has landed when a facet is new. A behaviour goes into that structure, never into the prose of a `description`; one the contract cannot say is signalled (`contract-gap-no-emulation.mdc`). The developer models it in a modeling session, which may read the code but writes none of it while it models: the agent proposes the contract with the patterns that make it good and shows `bun run contract:diff main` with the diff, each decision is a commit of its own on top, never an amend or a re-cut, so the developer follows it as its own step, and the card names its trunk commits and the developer's word that its contract is right, which is the OK. The implementation (the code, its tests and the e2e tests, their scaffold included, and the docs that quote the code) starts only after the OK, on the area branch, in commits of its own on top of the card's take, which copies the commits the developer approved and so needs no stop of its own; any session may write it, the modeling session included. The contract and the code are never written at the same time, and the code never before its contract. The OK covers the card's trunk commits, and the one commit it approves in advance: the last business commit of a rename or a removal, written on the area branch, which drops the old names the card listed and nothing else. A contract change found during the implementation goes back to the first phase: asked of the modeling session, committed on the trunk, approved there, and taken as one more commit. A business commit written on an area branch instead, a feature begun there before the trunk or an amendment the developer lets an implementer write there, stops for review: hand the developer the contract diff (`bun run contract:diff main`, the diff of those commits named by hash, the meta landing they build on, the reason for each change, the gaps signalled), ask for the OK on those commits, and wait. A change that review brings, one the developer asks for or one an answer to their question leads to, is a commit of its own on top of the ones under review, never an amend or a re-cut; the OK is then asked on all of them. Any other contract commit made, re-cut, amended or rebased after that OK, even with its files unchanged, and a return to the implementation after other work, stop again, and no commit of the implementation stays on the branch without an OK on the contract commits beneath it. The gate holds whenever the intended behaviour changes or the contract needs a correction or an extension; a fix that brings the code back to what the contract already says, correctly, needs no contract diff and no stop. A screen's transient state comes from its flow run as its machine, never from a component's own flags.
- **The files.** Authored: `contract/vocabulary.md`, `contract/domain/screens/<pathname>.yaml` (`__root.yaml` for the frame), `contract/domain/platform.yaml`, `contract/domain/model.schema.json`, `contract/domain/activities/<id>.yaml`. Generated, never edited: `screen.schema.json`, `platform.schema.json`, `activity.schema.json`, `x-perch.schema.json`, `manifest.schema.json` (`bun run contract:schemas`) and the block of `contract/COVERAGE.md`. A word the vocabulary lacks is added to it in the same change. Every description a domain file writes, the maps of meanings included (a screen's `states`, the platform's `events`, the sentence guards of a `meta.guards`), says what its element is for and leaves to the structure, another element, the platform, the model, the vocabulary and the flow what they say, as the schema text of its field puts it, the hint an editor shows; each keeps 200 characters at most, whatever it describes (`contract/README.md`, "Descriptions"). A platform action that sends a mail declares it (`mail`: `to`, `language`, `link`); scenarios name it `[mail:<action>]`, and a test holds a mail it reads to it. A mail that only tells, with no token, is a notice the action lists under `notices` (`name`, `to`, `language`, an optional plain `link` to a screen whose routes need no parameter), named `[notice:<action>.<name>]`.
- **Grammar.** An anchor (`data-testid`) is 2 to 4 dot-separated kebab-case segments, a screen name first and a kind last (`user.sign-in.email-input`); it says what the element is for, never its text, colour, position or an entity's id.
- **One literal per rendered name**, declared once in one screen file and written once in the sources; no anchor equals another plus a derived suffix (`device.list.session-item`, never `device.list.item`). Four literal forms only: `data-testid="..."`, a `testId` or `<part>TestId` prop, a static `testId: '...'` key, a `<NAME>_TEST_ID` constant; never built at runtime.
- **Derived names only through `testIdFor`.** A composite takes one `testId` and builds its parts with `testIdFor(testId, '<suffix>')`, the suffix from the vocabulary and in the base's `derived`; a part with a screen-level name of its own is a named prop (`navigationTestId`).
- **Placement.** On the semantic element whose role the kind maps to, never on a wrapper, a fragment, a portal or a backdrop; a leaf kit component spreads its props, so the call site writes the literal.
- **What needs one.** A container that scopes a test, every entry of a list, every control a test or an agent drives (every label is translated), a value a test reads, every state and message; not decorations, wrappers or an icon inside a marked control. A control that is not a test target carries a coverage-ignore marker, never a made-up anchor; an icon-only control also carries an `aria-label`.
- **Codes, states and values, never wording.** An error's `fields` name the field each of its codes concerns, which it shows under and marks invalid. `data-code` on a `message`, `error`, `toast` or `dialog` (a dialog's code names what it asks), `data-state` on a container or an entry, `data-value` with the key of a choice that is not an entity. A behaviour that varies is a code or a state, never a second anchor; a message whose codes more than one source may raise before it shows says `ranked: true` and lists its `codes` in rank order, so of those raised it shows the first; a domain code is mapped to a contract code in the presentation layer. A field that holds a secret (a password, its confirmation) says `secret: true`, never travels in an address (no navigate's search carries it, no search parameter starts it), and alone may render a `visibility-button`. A field holds input unless its own change runs its action, which the loader derives (`holdsInput`); the element of a field that holds none carries `holdsInput={false}`, and the sources check holds it to the contract. Every leave that would lose unsaved input is declared in `platform.yaml` under `unsavedInput` with how it asks (through the frame's dialog, by the browser, never, or it cannot); the browser's own prompt is drawn outside the page and has no anchor.
- **Repeated items** share one static anchor with `data-id`; an entry that differs or changes in place carries a row state, a normal one none. **A composite mounted on its own is not a singleton**: the shell's instance owns the reserved anchor, every other mount passes its own `testId`, and a feature never renders a reserved anchor.
- **Scenarios** are Markdown files, `tests/e2e/scenarios/<entity>/<layer>/[<area>/]<slug>.md` beside `tests/e2e/src/<same path>.e2e.spec.ts`, whose brackets name what the contract declares (an anchor, a route, `[code:<code>]`, `[state:<state>]`, `[value:<value>]`, `[mail:<action>]`, `[notice:<action>.<name>]`; a code, a state or a value belongs to the anchor its text names last before it, a route read past and a mail or a notice ending the pairing, and is one that anchor or an anchor under it carries, so the anchor comes first), and the contract's feedback loop: a scenario that cannot be written from the contract alone names a gap to fix in the contract files, never one to work around in a test; a gap the contract cannot close in the change is signalled, never emulated (`contract-gap-no-emulation.mdc`).
- **Routes.** A binding is a TanStack Router route and a state address, whose `roles` are the roles it serves: the router serves it only to an account that holds one of them, after its `guard` and before it reads the search, and opens the screen of the binding at `/$`, the not-found screen, at the same address for any other (`contract/README.md`, "Roles"); `apps/web/src/platform/routes-contract.spec.ts` and `routes-router.spec.ts` hold the module routes and the real router to it, and `routes-examples.spec.ts` its example link (`bun run contract:routes`) and the route tree.
- **Flows.** A screen's `flow` is an XState v5 machine config in the subset `setup({ guards, actions, actors }).createMachine(flow)` takes as written (the route owns the addressable state, the machine the transient one, save whether a password input shows what it holds, which the input keeps and its derived `visibility-button` toggles, never an event of a flow and always enabled; the screen's independent areas are the regions of a parallel state, each event handled by one area, a region's state leaving for a state of the root by `#<flow id>.<state>`, the flow's `id` a one-segment name the flow declares, which the app gives its machine; an event is an anchor acted on, a platform event, or a data event `model.<entity>.<field>` of a field the screen shows; a control whose anchor is an event (a button, a select, a switch) is enabled exactly in the states that handle it, read from the machine, never from a flag; an anchor only some states render is listed in `meta.renders` of those states, a region's state rendering its parallel state's list too, one no state lists is rendered in every state, a listed anchor renders the anchors declared under it (`<anchor>.<rest>`, a list's items) with it, never listed beside them, and a state that handles an event on a listed anchor, or on one under it, renders it; rendered means visible to the user, an anchor a state does not render being absent from the page or hidden on it, and the app reads what its state renders from its machine (`renderedAt`, `isRendered`), never from a flag, while an e2e run holds the page to it in every settled state (`settledProblems`); a guard of `meta.guards` is a sentence, or the failure codes it takes, `{ codes: [ … ] }`, which guards only branches of an invoke's `onError`; a list whose `source` is a platform action reads it each time it starts to render after it did not and each time the organisation or account it reads for or a search parameter its source takes becomes another (its `params`, each `{ search }` or a constant, never a field; `paged: true` shows the page and the size the route's `page` and `size` name and derives `range`, `previous-link`, `next-link` and `size-select`, whose options are the controls of `size`, so it writes no `x-perch`, and a navigate that sets a key it reads says `page: 1`), and every other read is the flow's effect `read: <list>`, a list of its own screen with a platform source, never an entity source, while an invoke after which nothing reads says `read: false`, and one branch of an invoke's `onDone` or `onError` after which nothing reads, where another branch reads, says `read: false` itself, never the transition of an event, never beside its own read and never under an invoke that says it, a statement the checks and the diff read and the machine comparison leaves out), and `contract:check` runs every flow and activity in XState, while an app holds its own machine for a screen to its flow with the engine's comparison (the web app's `apps/web/src/platform/machines.spec.ts`). A screen file may write its flow shorter, a transition as its target, an effect by its name (`show: <anchor>`), a region by its pattern (`draft`) or by its button (`by: <button>`), which the loader expands into that config before anything reads it (`bun run contract:flow <screen>` prints it); a shape one region has is written in full until a second shares it. **What the contract says elsewhere, a screen leaves out**, and the loader derives it the same way every time (`contract/README.md`, "What a screen leaves out"): what an action fails with (the platform's codes; for the model, `forbidden` under `x-perch.can`, the codes of the constraints of the fields it sets, `failed`), a button's params that the fields of its part pass to its action, an error's `codes` and `fields` from the actions its part binds, the actions its flow invokes against it and the codes the flow shows on it, the codes a `show: <anchor>` in an `onError` shows (on a branch guarded by failure code the guard's codes, on a later one the failure's other codes), the failure of a request (an invoke failing with codes and writing no `onError` clears its part's error on entry and goes back the one way it came, on a control, showing the failure there; a state where its area starts, one a platform or data event enters, or one with no single way back writes it, and one written as its target alone gets the show on its part's error, else on `<screen>.error`, also when no control enters it and its input names no field), the whole region of a button bound to one action that the flow does not handle, the list and items of a composite of the platform, which a secret input opts into with `composite: <name>`, and the descriptions the engine says itself (a heading, a link to a screen, a secret input, an error, a composite's anchors; `contract:coverage --descriptions` lists the written ones to drop). The screen writes only what a tester or a user can tell apart; a written value wins over a derived one, and `contract:diff` lists the codes the anchors gain or lose, derived ones included, and the lines of each flow, its states' addresses, what they render, what each guard by failure code takes and a flow added or removed whole included, so a screen written shorter that loads the same shows no change but the descriptions it stops writing; it lists last every written description added, removed or changed by its place, with its length before and after (`app-shell.theme-select 191 -> 340`, `new -> 120`, `340 -> gone`), so a description that grows shows on review.
- **Activities.** A task across screens is an activity, `contract/domain/activities/<id>.yaml`: an XState machine config whose states are visits to screens (a dot-free name, `meta.screen`, `meta.address` a binding of that screen) and whose transitions on an anchor of that screen or the frame the screens' flows or links must back, which a scenario that walks it names in its `activity`.
- **The checks**, in `bun run check` and the pre-commit hook: `contract:check` (the files, their cross-references, who each route serves, a paged list's action and route, the schema drift, the scenarios' brackets, each code, state or value with its anchor, every written description within 200 characters, one past it named with the purpose of its field), `contract:check:sources` (the JSX of `apps/web/src` and `packages/ui/src`), `contract:check:consumers` (the literals of `tests/e2e/src`, a code, state or value asserted on an anchor with that anchor). On a branch the hook lets the contract run ahead of the app (`contract:check:sources:ahead`: the app uses only what the contract declares, and an anchor declared but not rendered yet passes; and the unit tests with `CONTRACT_AHEAD=1`: an app's machine may lack what its flow added, a state, an event, a guarded branch, an effect, an anchor a state renders, but does nothing the flow does not say, or is still exactly its screen's flow at the branch's merge base with `main` (`CONTRACT_AHEAD_BASE`) or at the commit the branch is at (`CONTRACT_AHEAD_HEAD`, the contract the app was built from, for a card's second business commit taken onto a branch), and a route may still read as its binding did at that merge base, so a business commit may change a transition or a binding the app has not changed yet; the hook names both commits, so the unit tests' cache keys on them; and a scenario may wait for its test, as the hook leaves `e2e:coverage` out); a commit on `main` and `bun run check` take the full check, `e2e:coverage` and `contract:coverage:check` (the block of `contract/COVERAGE.md` as `contract:coverage --write` writes it) included, so nothing lands unrendered, half-built, untested or with its coverage block behind. A rename or a removal therefore goes in three commits: the business commit adds the new name and keeps the old one (on the trunk, or with the review stop on an area branch), the implementation switches to it, a last business commit on the area branch drops the old one, approved with the first. On `contract/trunk` the hook first holds each commit, a merge aside, to the business layer: one that stages a file outside `contract/domain/`, `contract/vocabulary.md` (taken whole, an edit of its grammar left to the review of the trunk's diff) and `tests/e2e/scenarios/` fails, naming each such file (`scripts/contract-trunk.ts`). On an area branch the commit-msg hook holds a commit that changes the business layer to the form of a take, `<type>(contract): <the card's goal>` with `Taken-from: contract/trunk <first>^..<last> <card>`, or of a business commit written there, with `Written-on-branch: <card>`: that layer alone, and a take's commits on the trunk's own line, changing every file it stages (`scripts/contract-take.ts`, `branch-reuse-first.mdc`); and the hook first refuses a commit while a hook it names is not installed (`scripts/hooks-installed.ts`). Coverage measures the model first, then the controls; a control off it carries `// ui-contract-ignore[-self]: <reason>` or `{/* ui-contract-ignore[-self]: <reason> */}` right before its element.
- **The manifest** is built only after the check passes and served by every stand at `/ui-manifest/manifest.json` (`PERCH_UI_MANIFEST=0` turns it off); development and e2e builds add `window.__perch.ui.anchors()`. A contract change hands over `bun run contract:diff main` with the reason for each rename or removal.
